WEBVTT

00:00:00.635 --> 00:00:24.403
<v Casey>Morning, good to have you. Friday's the day we dig into Recreation and Backcountry Access, and today's a good one for it. We've got a burned-out slope near Clinton where the flood and slide warnings are now preliminary risk maps, and the question of who signs off on reopening a road. Plus Rohr Lake in fresh snow, a hiker who couldn't tell if the access road was safe, and the Cariboo Chilcotin Forestry Forum. Stay with us.

00:00:37.878 --> 00:00:57.734
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Friday, November 13th, 2026. Today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life. We're broadcasting from the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:27.006
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today is about getting out into wild country, and about the trails, roads and rules that decide how we get there. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's partly cloudy and around 6 degrees, with a high of 16 and a low of 5. It turns overcast through the day with no precipitation expected. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 6, Williams Lake around 9, Quesnel around 4 and Alexis Creek around 6, all partly cloudy.

00:01:27.392 --> 00:01:29.528
<v Casey>Dress for the overcast, and on to the news.

00:01:30.928 --> 00:01:36.592
<v Riley>Most of today's news is about land and who answers for it, and a good share of it is close to home.

00:01:38.032 --> 00:02:04.632
<v Casey>The Cariboo Regional District swore in its area directors yesterday, November 12th, with Provincial Court Judge Linda Thomas administering the oaths. Newly elected, re-elected and acclaimed directors all took the oath together. The acclaimed directors were Nicole Audet in Area A, Steve Forseth in Area D, Maureen LeBourdais in Area F, and the Area I director. Judge Thomas told the new board that some decisions would be tougher than others, and that they would need to work together on them.

00:02:05.082 --> 00:02:09.210
<v Riley>Four seats went uncontested, so those four directors took office without a vote.

00:02:10.924 --> 00:02:47.242
<v Riley>Nearly 70 people from First Nations, local government and industry met in Williams Lake on November 6th for the second Cariboo Chilcotin Forestry Forum. The City of Williams Lake hosted it, building on a July gathering. Beth Veenkamp, the city's manager of economic development, said that bringing people into one room is where connections and opportunities start. She also called forestry a big ship that is hard to turn, so the aim is to find local solutions to local problems. Percy Guichon of Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation opened the day with a talk on the Palmer Project.

00:02:47.691 --> 00:02:51.907
<v Casey>Convening is cheap. The test is whether anything gets written down afterward.

00:02:53.633 --> 00:03:25.579
<v Casey>That same company, Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation Ltd., won the 2026 Cando Indigenous Private Sector Business Award, presented at the national conference in Victoria from October 5th to 8th. CCR is the joint venture of the three Tŝilhqot'in communities we named yesterday. Its work covers forest rehabilitation, wildfire risk reduction and local jobs across the Cariboo Chilcotin. CEO Percy Guichon, who is also an elected councillor of Tŝideldel First Nation, said the award recognizes the crews, communities and partners who got the work done.

00:03:25.579 --> 00:03:29.659
<v Riley>Rehab on damaged ground is the other half of what happens after a fire.

00:03:31.403 --> 00:03:50.419
<v Riley>Quesnel marked Remembrance Day on Wednesday, November 11th. A parade and muster started around 10:45 in the morning, and the community gathered at the cenotaph beside the local Legion branch. Poppies and wreaths were laid in honour of those who served and sacrificed in wartime and peacekeeping.

00:03:52.015 --> 00:04:27.349
<v Casey>From the cenotaph to the grassland. Specialists in Sweden, France and South Africa are arguing that grazing should follow the grass rather than the calendar. Sweden's national inventory found that the natural and cultural values of most re-surveyed meadows and pastures stayed broadly stable. In France, the Pâtur'ajuste network, started in 2011, counts 450 livestock farmers across 65 departments. Lesego Motshekga, a pasture scientist at the University of Limpopo, says rotational grazing only works when stocking rates match the forage. Moving animals between paddocks can't prevent overgrazing on its own.

00:04:27.799 --> 00:04:34.175
<v Riley>Grazing by the grass is a harder rule to write into a permit, but ranchers here already do it by eye.

00:04:35.986 --> 00:05:05.562
<v Riley>The Global Carbon Project's annual assessment, published November 10th, projects land-use emissions of 5.1 billion tonnes this year. Peat fires and forest degradation in Southeast Asia, linked to El Niño, are the main driver. Total global CO2 is projected at 43.3 billion tonnes, even as fossil-fuel emissions edge down. The land carbon sink rebounded in 2025, but the strong 2026 El Niño is expected to weaken it again in 2027.

00:05:06.012 --> 00:05:09.428
<v Casey>So fossil fuels dipped, and burning land still pushed the total up.

00:05:11.096 --> 00:05:52.590
<v Casey>Researchers traced arsenic and heavy metals in ten Arctic ice cores to the Anaconda Copper Mine in Montana. In some cores arsenic reached about 15 times preindustrial levels around 1914. Combining the ice records with atmospheric modelling and production records, the team estimated that about 50.5 million Americans and 600,000 Canadians were exposed to arsenic levels linked to higher health risk between 1898 and 1918. Pollution controls installed in 1919 cut emissions by roughly 60 to 65 percent, but the mine kept running until 1947. Lead researcher Joseph McConnell says epidemiologists should look at possible health effects.

00:05:54.198 --> 00:06:04.550
<v Riley>About 300 glaciers have disappeared from the American West since the middle of the 20th century. A CleanTechnica piece looks at what the survivors have in common.

00:06:05.971 --> 00:06:29.915
<v Casey>A Caltech study in Science Advances found that smoke from the Eaton Fire in Los Angeles carried lead at roughly 80 times normal atmospheric levels for 24 hours. About 7 million residents were under that plume. The researchers say the smoke came mainly from burning homes, vehicles and infrastructure, so its chemistry differs from forest smoke. The plume also carried chlorine, bromine, zinc, copper and arsenic.

00:06:30.365 --> 00:06:36.365
<v Riley>Most air-quality advice was built around forest smoke, and burning towns put out a different chemistry.

00:06:38.046 --> 00:07:08.302
<v Riley>New EPA data shows 1,907 tonnes of farmed salmon died in Macquarie Harbour, on Tasmania's west coast, between October 2024 and December 2025. Salmon Tasmania says that's about 8 percent of the fish farmed there over that period. The numbers came out through a Right to Information request by Environment Tasmania, and only after the salmon companies asked for reviews of the release. The EPA has not yet issued new licences for the harbour's 10 salmon leases.

00:07:08.752 --> 00:07:11.944
<v Casey>Eight percent is the kind of number a licence renewal should be built on.

00:07:13.675 --> 00:07:36.707
<v Casey>A map from More Than Just Parks, a publication that tracks AI data centres on public land, counts at least 14 proposed data centres within ten miles of a U.S. national park boundary. Another 38 sit within 25 miles, and 131 within 50 miles. Will Pattiz, a cofounder of the project, says many of these are being pushed through with secrecy, so people don't know who is building them.

00:07:38.149 --> 00:07:55.117
<v Riley>One of those fights played out in Bonner, Montana, a former mill town of about 2,700 people on the Blackfoot River, known for trout fishing and whitewater. In March, an Idaho-based company called Krambu announced plans for an AI data centre near the river. Residents pushed back.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:08:18.346
<v Casey>Over the Thanksgiving long weekend, one hiker made a one-night backpacking trip to Rohr Lake. The hike is 4.5 kilometres with 500 metres of elevation gain, done in fresh snow and about minus 5. They started at the trailhead just past Joffre Lakes. The hiker parked at the very bottom of the access road because they weren't sure of its condition, and they only had the one car.

00:08:18.796 --> 00:08:22.020
<v Riley>An uncertain road added distance before the trail even started.

00:08:23.794 --> 00:08:45.890
<v Riley>Young smǝlqmíx people rallied in Keremeos on Sunday, November 8th, against the New Ingerbelle pit expansion at the Copper Mountain Mine. The province issued Mines Act and Environmental Management Act permits for the expansion in February. The nmlqitkʷ Similkameen River Defenders say the project threatens the river, and more than 50 people came out to the event.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:50.580
<v Casey>The permits are already issued, so the fight now is over conditions and enforcement.

00:08:52.358 --> 00:09:15.814
<v Casey>Myanmar's peatlands are a recent find. Scientists identified more than 200,000 hectares in 2024, a figure 1,718 percent above earlier estimates. The civil war, now in its fifth year, has made the peat nearly impossible to map, protect or monitor. International funders withdrew protection projects citing political instability, and officials warn the delay could be irreversible.

00:09:16.264 --> 00:09:20.352
<v Riley>Peatland loss can't be undone, so a stalled plan is a decision too.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:37.414
<v Riley>The U.S. Senate voted 73 to 25 on Monday to take up a sprawling permitting bill that would overhaul major environmental laws to streamline the process for energy projects. The bill has divided Democrats. The story is linked in the episode notes.

00:09:37.863 --> 00:09:41.143
<v Casey>Fast permits cut both ways, for pipelines and power lines alike.

00:09:42.817 --> 00:09:53.473
<v Casey>Fire, flood and smoke have run through several of today's stories. Before we get to what burned ground does once the flames are out, a quick word about people who build the ground we travel on.

00:09:54.873 --> 00:10:17.209
<v Casey>If you've ever wondered what a well-built trail takes, First Journey Trails designs and builds them, and runs the Indigenous Youth Mountain Bike Program. Young people come into the trail work from the start, through design and construction, and then they ride what they built. It's practical, and the result is something you can roll a tire over. You can learn more at firstjourneytrails dot com.

00:10:17.528 --> 00:10:21.632
<v Riley>Building the trail and riding it in the same program is a smart pairing.

00:10:21.933 --> 00:10:23.789
<v Casey>Now to the burned country near Clinton.

00:10:25.189 --> 00:10:36.637
<v Casey>When the flames go out, the danger stays. Near Clinton, the burned ground is still flooding and sliding. So is that country open, or is it hazardous? And for a road or a creek bank, who gets to say which one it is?

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:46.132
<v Riley>Open, with conditions posted. A hazard report is a tool. It lets people reopen burned country in stages instead of shutting whole areas indefinitely.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:52.524
<v Casey>The floods and slides are already happening. A warning that more are to come is arriving after the hazard started.

00:10:53.170 --> 00:11:08.034
<v Riley>It's arriving early enough to act on. The provincial preliminary risk analysis from the Ministry of Forests, reported by CBC on November 12th, found a high risk of landslides, floods and debris flow in parts of the Cariboo after the Pear Lake wildfire.

00:11:08.621 --> 00:11:20.197
<v Casey>That fire burned more than 1,600 square kilometres near Clinton, about 166,000 hectares. It was the largest B.C. fire this year, and it destroyed more than 100 structures.

00:11:20.732 --> 00:11:36.108
<v Riley>Hundreds of evacuation orders went out too. The report names roads, railways and properties as the most exposed assets, and it singles out Highway 97, the CN Rail corridor and Pavilion-Clinton Road. That's a list a regional district can act on.

00:11:36.434 --> 00:11:40.482
<v Casey>A list comes before a decision. Somebody still has to make the decision.

00:11:41.083 --> 00:11:48.723
<v Riley>And the clock on it is known. The Thompson-Nicola Regional District says the risk is highest in the first two years after a fire.

00:11:49.379 --> 00:11:56.595
<v Casey>Two years. And the detailed report is due in December, so the people on those roads get the full picture after the fall rains have already hit.

00:11:57.282 --> 00:12:06.354
<v Riley>Which is why the preliminary one matters now. Officials say the risk rises with rain forecasts and with the spring freshet. Those are triggers you can watch for.

00:12:07.011 --> 00:12:11.723
<v Casey>Watch for them how? A household on a gravel road doesn't have a hydrologist on speed dial.

00:12:12.274 --> 00:12:28.050
<v Riley>They have the village. The Village of Clinton issued an Evacuation Alert for flood risk back in August. Most of it was rescinded on August 24th, but properties along Highway 97 between LeBourdais Avenue and the ambulance station stayed under alert.

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:29.086
<v Casey>Why those blocks?

00:12:29.362 --> 00:12:41.698
<v Riley>Clinton Creek passes under an undersized CN Railway culvert there. If debris plugs it, the water backs up toward the houses. That's a local authority putting a line on the map before the water arrives.

00:12:42.288 --> 00:12:48.232
<v Casey>An alert tells you which blocks to worry about. It doesn't tell you which turn on a back road is safe tomorrow.

00:12:48.752 --> 00:13:05.416
<v Riley>Then look at what the ground does to water. Scorched soil is water-repellent, so rain runs off the surface instead of soaking in. And the fire destroyed the natural drainage, so that runoff goes straight into the channels. Officials tie the increase in risk to exactly those two things.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:07.984
<v Casey>So the fire took the trees and the sponge.

00:13:08.529 --> 00:13:14.057
<v Riley>Right. A storm that a healthy slope would absorb turns into a debris flow on burned ground.

00:13:14.742 --> 00:13:25.970
<v Casey>And it already has. Near Clinton, soot and debris piled up behind a creek dam and made a road impassable for about a day. There were further landslides a few kilometres away. Nobody had to forecast those.

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:31.106
<v Riley>And people responded on the ground. B.C. Hydro crews helped neighbours divert possible slides.

00:13:31.628 --> 00:13:37.532
<v Casey>Diverting a slide protects one property. It doesn't tell a driver whether the next culvert is going to hold.

00:13:38.042 --> 00:13:53.066
<v Riley>Different job, and the province has a documented method for it. Land Management Handbook No. 69, published in 2015, ties risk to burn severity, to gentle terrain sitting above steep ground, and to burned areas upslope of creek channels.

00:13:53.582 --> 00:14:04.822
<v Casey>Those same factors came out of the 2003 and 2007 southeastern B.C. fires. A review of 36 debris-flow and flood events found them then. The knowledge is more than a decade old.

00:14:05.385 --> 00:14:10.401
<v Riley>So the method exists. What's slow is applying it to a specific road or a specific creek.

00:14:11.015 --> 00:14:21.055
<v Casey>Slow for whom? The risk sits on creek beds, and a lot of creek beds run through private ranches. Who pays for the check dams, the culvert upgrades, the mapping of a single property?

00:14:21.363 --> 00:14:27.883
<v Riley>I don't know yet, and I'd rather say so than guess. The province and the landowners will have to work that out.

00:14:28.542 --> 00:14:34.454
<v Casey>Then the warning arrives without a funded fix. A risk map nobody can afford to act on is still just a map.

00:14:34.965 --> 00:14:44.093
<v Riley>It's a map that tells a rancher which creek to watch. That changes what a rancher does on a spring morning when the freshet starts, even with no check dam yet.

00:14:44.728 --> 00:14:51.344
<v Casey>Or it tells a rancher the risk sits on their land and nobody else's. That's a heavy thing to be handed by a document.

00:14:51.918 --> 00:15:02.366
<v Riley>Which argues for putting the responsibility in writing, with a name on it. The detailed analysis in December should say which parcels, which roads, and who holds the call.

00:15:02.652 --> 00:15:05.860
<v Casey>That's a lot to ask from a report still labelled preliminary.

00:15:06.403 --> 00:15:14.747
<v Riley>I'm asking for a schedule. The province updates the hazard picture when the rain changes. The regional district posts the change at the road.

00:15:15.374 --> 00:15:24.958
<v Casey>And who checks that it happens? A promise to update is cheap in a board room. The report's warning is that more is to come, and nobody has said who writes the next reading.

00:15:25.261 --> 00:15:28.741
<v Riley>Someone at the province, and their office should be on the sign.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:34.860
<v Casey>Then take the default head on. After a megafire, should burned country be closed until it's mapped?

00:15:35.141 --> 00:15:49.109
<v Riley>No. Clinton sits right beside the burn. People need Highway 97 and Pavilion-Clinton Road to get to work, to get children to school, to reach the ranch. Closing everything indefinitely pushes them onto roads nobody has checked at all.

00:15:49.465 --> 00:15:52.121
<v Casey>So reopen on a snapshot, and call the snapshot safe?

00:15:52.446 --> 00:16:02.094
<v Riley>Reopen on conditions. Open when the slope is quiet, close when rain is forecast, and post both. People stay informed, and nobody pretends the ground is settled.

00:16:02.721 --> 00:16:12.537
<v Casey>That raises the sharper question. When a road reopens, should the people who did the clearing get back on it ahead of the people who just waited? Is it fair to put the helpers first?

00:16:12.848 --> 00:16:21.728
<v Riley>For the first pass, yes. The crews who moved the debris know where the water came through, and that knowledge is what the reopening decision needs.

00:16:22.358 --> 00:16:31.222
<v Casey>And the driver who lost a week of work to the closure? They didn't clear anything because nobody asked them, or they couldn't. They're the one paying for the wait.

00:16:31.875 --> 00:16:38.675
<v Riley>Then the crews go first to check it, and everyone goes second at the same time. Priority for the job, nothing beyond that.

00:16:39.196 --> 00:16:44.116
<v Casey>That's a narrower claim than "the helpers get looked after first." I can live with that one.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:52.446
<v Riley>It's how it worked here. B.C. Hydro crews helped neighbours who weren't their customers on that slope and weren't asked to pay anything back.

00:16:53.056 --> 00:17:00.184
<v Casey>Which is the best case for your side. But the neighbour who can't lift a shovel still needs the road open when the crew is done.

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:07.564
<v Riley>So the sign should say why the road is open and who checked it. Then every driver gets the same information, crew or not.

00:17:08.106 --> 00:17:12.498
<v Casey>Except a driver can't check a slope from a sign. They can only trust the name on it.

00:17:13.026 --> 00:17:22.098
<v Riley>Which is why the name matters. If the regional district or the province is named, someone has to answer for a road that gets called safe and then gives way.

00:17:22.686 --> 00:17:36.198
<v Casey>You've moved me on one thing. A closure with no end date is a hazard of its own for the people living out there, especially through two years of it. What you haven't shown me is that anyone will take a current reading before each reopening.

00:17:36.728 --> 00:17:41.976
<v Riley>Then we agree on the risk and disagree on the default. I still say conditions beat closure.

00:17:42.505 --> 00:17:48.865
<v Casey>And I say treat it as hazardous until somebody has walked the slope and signed for it. The ground is still moving.

00:17:49.191 --> 00:17:52.255
<v Riley>It's the Rohr Lake problem from up top, on bigger ground.

00:17:52.939 --> 00:18:03.299
<v Casey>That hiker could walk up and look. A burn scar's slope above a creek is hard to judge from a turnout, and the people who drive through it most often aren't the ones who've seen it from above.

00:18:03.952 --> 00:18:12.816
<v Riley>Which is why local knowledge has to be written down. Crews who clear a road should file what they found, and that report should feed the next update.

00:18:13.147 --> 00:18:15.875
<v Casey>A tool nobody updates is a warning with a nicer font.

00:18:16.207 --> 00:18:18.623
<v Riley>And I'm saying the font can be changed by Tuesday.

00:18:19.185 --> 00:18:24.097
<v Casey>Ha. Put the date on the sign, and the name under it. Then we can argue about the default.

00:18:24.726 --> 00:18:30.886
<v Riley>Deal. Conditions posted, dated, and signed by the people who can change them. That could work by freshet.

00:18:31.485 --> 00:18:46.965
<v Casey>It's unproven until someone shows me who's answerable when a slope called safe gives way. Right now that's a regional district, a province, and a December report. If you live near Clinton and know who actually checked your road, tell us at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:18:47.484 --> 00:18:53.956
<v Riley>And tell us whether the crews who cleared it should get first crack at it. Enjoy your weekend, everyone.

00:18:54.281 --> 00:18:56.297
<v Casey>Check the date on the sign before you drive.

00:18:56.896 --> 00:19:16.000
